insert into public.day_recaps (series_id, season_number, week, day, recap) values
('dark-moon', 1, 6, 1, 'The watchman who looked up at your window raised the alarm, and all night the Order''s lanterns hunted along the wall. Twice their boots went right past the hidden room, and twice they walked on, because from the square every slit in that wall looks the same. In the stone niche where the fifth had lain, you found a fragment, and under the slit window an arrow scratched long ago pointed down into the wall. At dusk the Order struck its board, and you struck the dead watchman''s page to six while Garrick watched. The hall is shut fast, but the Seekers left a way in somewhere. The arrow points down, to the defenders'' barracks under the wall.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 6, 2, 'In the morning the crossroads where the king''s soldiers had stood for centuries was empty. In the barracks under the wall you cleared a fallen beam, and one of the armoured dead came down the stair and stood in the doorway, looking past you at the stone. When the heartbeat came, it turned and climbed away up the hill. You found a fragment in the hearth of the small room where the Seekers slept, passed the rest of the soldiers three streets nearer the hall, and struck the page to five at dusk. The dead are not coming for you. They are going to him. Tonight, after dark, across the great square to his walls.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 6, 3, 'At dusk you struck the page to four, and after dark you went up to the great square, where twice as many watchmen now sat round the Order''s fire. Their board showed four strokes left, the same as your page. You crossed sixty paces of open ground to the hall''s great wall and laid a palm on it. The stone was warm, as if something behind it had been warm for a very long time, and the heartbeat came through it into your hand. There is no way in at the front. The hall''s back wall runs into the hill, so tomorrow, the cracked hill behind it.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 6, 4, 'By noon the sky was as dim as dusk, and on the cracked hill behind the hall you found one crack that ran straight, as if it had been ruled. One of the king''s soldiers stood in its mouth with its back to the hall, and you broke it. Deep inside you found a fragment, and the crack ended in blank rock beside a carving of a full moon with its whole face scored out. Garrick reminded you that the Seekers shut only the doors they knew about, so there is another door into the hall, and the Order has it. That night you struck the page to three. The same scored moon is cut high on the moon-tower at the city''s edge.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 6, 5, 'The heartbeat no longer waited for dusk, and by noon the ground trembled with it. At the top of the moon-tower, where the old sky-watchers cut every moon into the wall, you found the one cut deeper than the rest, the full moon struck out on the night he fell, with bare stone after it. Counting forward with a fingertip at their spacing, you came all the way round the room to the doorpost, and that same moon comes again in two nights. As the nearly full moon came up over the open roof, Garrick said quietly, “It always comes back,” and you struck the page to two. You have the hour, one piece of the map is left, and tomorrow is the last day.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 6, 6, 'The street to the hill was full of the king''s soldiers, walking slowly up to his hall, and you crossed between their ranks. At the mouth of the crack one of them had turned back, and you broke it where the last one fell. At the dead end the last fragment made the map whole, and it showed that the blank rock is a door, marked with one word, *here*, beside the scored moon. Back in the wall room you struck the page to one, and Garrick poured the last of his flask, with one cup more on the sill of the slit window. Tomorrow night, when the moon goes dark, you go through the Seekers'' door into his hall with the fifth in your hands.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 6, 7, 'On the last night the Seekers'' door in the hill opened for your blood, and the last stroke went through the watch-page as the moon began to go dark. You fought through the faithful and the hollowed in his hall and found the king asleep on the floor where he fell, with the four Stones glowing in a ring around him. Your blood did nothing to the fifth. Garrick said, “Let me,” and the fifth answered his blood, and its light pinned you where you knelt. Moon-blood was never a word for you. It was the king''s own title, and then his house''s, and Garrick is the last of that house and the Order is his. He had never lied to you, and his people killed Meira on the road. From his own whole copy of their book he read the untorn page, which ends “and the throne rise whole.” The fifth was never going to stop the waking. It finished it. He set the extra cup beside the king, because it had always been for him, and closed the king''s hand around the fifth. “You were the key,” he said. Then the moon went dark, and the king opened his eyes and looked at you, knowing Seeker blood. “You saved every child you were sent to save,” Garrick said.')
